<div class="sl-heading-wrapper level-h3"><h3 id="building-for-32-bit-or-arm">Building for 32-bit or ARM</h3><a class="sl-anchor-link" href="#building-for-32-bit-or-arm"><span aria-hidden="true" class="sl-anchor-icon"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="m12.11 15.39-3.88 3.88a2.52 2.52 0 0 1-3.5 0 2.47 2.47 0 0 1 0-3.5l3.88-3.88a1 1 0 1 0-1.42-1.42l-3.88 3.89a4.48 4.48 0 0 0 6.33 6.33l3.89-3.88a1 1 0 0 0-1.42-1.42m8.58-12.08a4.49 4.49 0 0 0-6.33 0l-3.89 3.88a1 1 0 1 0 1.42 1.42l3.88-3.88a2.52 2.52 0 0 1 3.5 0 2.47 2.47 0 0 1 0 3.5l-3.88 3.88a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.42 1 1 0 0 0 1.42 0l3.88-3.89a4.49 4.49 0 0 0 0-6.33M8.83 15.17a1 1 0 0 0 .71.29 1 1 0 0 0 .71-.29l4.92-4.92a1 1 0 1 0-1.42-1.42l-4.92 4.92a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.42"></path></svg></span><span class="sr-only" data-pagefind-ignore>Section titled “Building for 32-bit or ARM”</span></a></div>
<p>The Tauri CLI compiles your executable using your machine’s architecture by default.
Assuming that you’re developing on a 64-bit machine, the CLI will produce 64-bit applications.</p>
<p>If you need to support <strong>32-bit</strong> machines, you can compile your application with a <strong>different</strong> <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/rustc/platform-support.html">Rust target</a>
using the <code dir="auto">--target</code> flag:</p>
